{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2270 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d0eb462e4fc68be767dcd20eb080e12bc6f05b3714c5f6c7cb1fee5bde765c38" :edb/db-before-t 2269 :edb/db-after-t 2270 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535582 50 #inst "2026-10-14T15:36:34.972Z" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186054812 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519521 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186054813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519554 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186054813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519576 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186064314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519526 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186073746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519514 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186073746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519582 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186075709 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519086 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186075709 1078 #edb/ref 17592186519086 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186076076 1078 #edb/ref 17592186076079 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186076556 1078 #edb/ref 17592186076559 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186077468 1078 #edb/ref 17592186077472 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078171 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of  $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{8a}}.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078171 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{8a}}$." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078171 1074 "First, we apply the quotient rule and put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8a}} &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8a}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{8a}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{8a}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8a}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{8a}}{\\sqrt{8a}} &=  \\\\\n(\\sqrt{24a})/((\\sqrt{8a})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt{4 \\cdot 6a}}{8a} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2\\sqrt{6a}}{8a} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{6a}}{4a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078171 1074 "First, we apply the quotient rule and put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8a}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8a}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{8a}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{8a}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8a}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{8a}}{\\sqrt{8a}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{24a}}{(\\sqrt{8a})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{4\\cdot 6a}}{8a} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{6a}}{8a} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6a}}{4a}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078172 1074 "We need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{z+1}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{z+1}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{y} + 2}{\\sqrt{z + 1}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt{y} + 2}{\\sqrt{z + 1}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{z + 1}}{\\sqrt{z + 1}} &=  \\\\\n((\\sqrt{y} + 2)\\sqrt{z + 1})/((\\sqrt{z + 1})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt{y} \\cdot \\sqrt{z + 1} + 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{z + 1}}{z + 1} &=  \\\\\n(\\sqrt{y \\cdot (z + 1)} + 2\\sqrt{z + 1})/(z + 1) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{yz + y} + 2\\sqrt{z + 1}}{z + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078172 1074 "We need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{z+1}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{z+1}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{\\sqrt{z+1}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{z+1}}{\\sqrt{z+1}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(\\sqrt{y}+2)\\sqrt{z+1}}{(\\sqrt{z+1})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{y}\\cdot \\sqrt{z+1}+2\\cdot \\sqrt{z+1}}{z+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{y\\cdot (z+1)}+2\\sqrt{z+1}}{z+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{yz+y}+2\\sqrt{z+1}}{z+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078179 1071 "Which of the three sequences below are arithmetic sequences?\n\n1. $2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: \\ldots$\n2. $5, \\: 2, \\: -1, \\: -4, \\: \\ldots$\n3. $2, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: \\ldots$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078179 1071 "Which of the three sequences below are arithmetic sequences?\n\nI. $2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: \\ldots$\n\nII. $5, \\: 2, \\: -1, \\: -4, \\: \\ldots$\n\nIII. $2, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: \\ldots$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078179 1074 "To determine whether each sequence is an arithmetic sequence, we need to look at the jumps between terms in the sequence. If the jumps are the same, then they indeed represent a common difference of an arithmetic sequence.\n\n- In sequence I, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence I is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=2$.\n\n![](images/17/17933a1cabd8d487dd1607f7ae7064d6e186e3454a52497977f866150c491f25.png)\n\n- In sequence II, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence II is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-3$.\n\n![](images/9d/9d9a41035215deb27d0578123a859d9b492b505d79b0a0a71f62c822942298ca.png)\n\n- In sequence III, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence III is not an arithmetic sequence.\n\n![](images/2b/2b860338be6ff8876c1368ed5f08162096873fa9992a9e88d7ff545752a5bd36.png)\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences I and II are arithmetic sequences." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078179 1074 "To determine whether each sequence is an arithmetic sequence, we need to look at the jumps between terms in the sequence. If the jumps are the same, then they indeed represent a common difference of an arithmetic sequence.\n\n- In sequence I, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence I is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=2.$\n\n![](images/17/17933a1cabd8d487dd1607f7ae7064d6e186e3454a52497977f866150c491f25.png)\n\n- In sequence II, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence II is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-3.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d9a41035215deb27d0578123a859d9b492b505d79b0a0a71f62c822942298ca.png)\n\n- In sequence III, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence III is not an arithmetic sequence.\n\n![](images/2b/2b860338be6ff8876c1368ed5f08162096873fa9992a9e88d7ff545752a5bd36.png)\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences I and II are arithmetic sequences." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078180 1071 "What is the $8$th term of the following arithmetic sequence?\n\n$$\n4, \\: 7, \\: 10, \\: 13, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078180 1071 "What is the $8$th term of the following arithmetic sequence?\n$4, \\: 7, \\: 10, \\: 13, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078180 1074 "![](images/ac/ac878a3d258fa19641d983863580af953bbea593b49bffff16f2f09eb7b6ab2d.png)\n\nWe notice that the sequence is an arithmetic sequence with a common difference of\n$d=7-4=3$.\n\nSo, we can calculate each term in the sequence by adding $3$ to the previous term.\n\nWe will continue listing terms of the sequence until we get to the $8$th term. Listing the terms up to the $8$th term, we have:\n\n![](images/e5/e536739b28db7d0f221967ce8729eee502a29f80caec8e1c39f61a9cfe4a6459.png)\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is $25$." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078180 1074 "![](images/ac/ac878a3d258fa19641d983863580af953bbea593b49bffff16f2f09eb7b6ab2d.png)\n\nWe notice that the sequence is an arithmetic sequence with a common difference of \n\n$$\nd=7-4=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we can calculate each term in the sequence by adding $3$ to the previous term. \n\nWe will continue listing terms of the sequence until we get to the $8$th term. Listing the terms up to the $8$th term, we have:\n\n![](images/e5/e536739b28db7d0f221967ce8729eee502a29f80caec8e1c39f61a9cfe4a6459.png)\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is $25.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078181 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $3$ and the common difference is $2$." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078181 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $3$ and the common difference is $2.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078181 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n= a_{1}+(n-1)d\n$$\n\nWe are told that the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\nd={\\color{red}2}\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\[3pt] &= {\\color{blue}3} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}2}) \\\\[3pt] &= 3 + 2n -2 \\\\[3pt] & =2n+1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n = 2n+1\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078181 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n= a_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}3}$ and the common difference is $d={\\color{red}2}.$ So, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ta_n\t&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n\t\t\t&= {\\color{blue}3} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}2})  \\[3pt]\n\t\t\t&= 3 + 2n -2 \\[3pt]\n\t\t\t& =2n+1\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore the formula for the $n$th term is $a_n = 2n+1.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078182 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $0$ and the second term is $-2$. Express your answer in function notation." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078182 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $0$ and the second term is $-2.$ Express your answer in function notation." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078182 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)= f(1)+(n-1)d\n$$\n\nWe are given the first term\n\n$$\nf(1)={\\color{blue}0}\n$$\n\nAlso, the common difference of the sequence can be found as\n\n$$\nd=-2 - 0 = {\\color{red}-2}\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(n)&= f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\[3pt] &= {\\color{blue}0} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}-2}) \\\\[3pt] &= 0 - 2n + 2 \\\\[3pt] &= 2 - 2n \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n) = 2 - 2n\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078182 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)= f(1)+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe are given the first term $f(1)={\\color{blue}0}.$ Also, the common difference of the sequence can be found as\n\n$$\nd=-2 - 0 = {\\color{red}-2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf(n)&= f(1)+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n&= {\\color{blue}0} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}-2})  \\[3pt]\n&= 0 - 2n + 2 \\[3pt]\n&= 2 - 2n\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is $f(n) = 2 - 2n.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078183 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive ($a_{12}$ goes right after $a_{11}$ in the sequence), we can find the common difference by subtracting $a_{11}$ from $a_{12}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{12} - a_{11} \\\\\n&= 34 - 39 \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, our result is negative, so we can be fairly confident that it is correct." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078183 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive ($a_{12}$ goes right after $a_{11}$ in the sequence), we can find the common difference by subtracting $a_{11}$ from $a_{12}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{12}-{a}_{11} \\\\  & =34-39 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, our result is negative, so we can be fairly confident that it is correct." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078184 1071 "Find the $50$th term of the following arithmetic sequence.\n\n$$\n-5, \\: -8, \\: -11, \\: \\ldots\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078184 1071 "Find the $50$th term of the following arithmetic sequence.\n$-5, \\: -8, \\: -11, \\: \\ldots$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078184 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n= a_{1}+(n-1)d\n$$\n\nFirst, we will write the formula for the $n$th term of this sequence. From the first few terms\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}-5}, \\: -8, \\: -11, \\ldots\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}-5}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\nd=-8-(-5)={\\color{red}-3}\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\[3pt] &= {\\color{blue}-5} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}-3}) \\\\[3pt] & =-5 -3n + 3 \\\\[3pt] &=-3n-2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use our formula to find the $50$th term. All we have to do is substitute $n=50$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &=-3n-2 \\\\[7pt] a_{50} &= -3(50)-2 \\\\[3pt] &= -150-2 \\\\[3pt] &= -152 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $50$th term of the sequence is\n\n$$\na_{50}=-152\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078184 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\na_n= a_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nFirst, we will write the formula for the $n$th term of this sequence. From the first few terms\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}-5}, \\: -8, \\: -11, \\ldots,\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}-5}$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=-8-(-5)={\\color{red}-3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n&= {\\color{blue}-5} + (n - 1)({\\color{red}-3})  \\[3pt]\n& =-5 -3n + 3 \\[3pt]\n&=-3n-2\n}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use our formula to find the $50$th term. All we have to do is substitute $n=50\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_n &=-3n-2 \\[7pt]\na_{50} &= -3(50)-2 \\[3pt]\n&= -150-2 \\[3pt]\n&= -152\t\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $50$th term of the sequence is $a_{50}=-152.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078185 1071 "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if the $12$th term is $-56$ and the $20$th term is $-112$." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078185 1071 "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if the $12$th term is $-56$ and the $20$th term is $-112.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078185 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\na_{\\color{black}12} = {\\color{red}-56}, \\quad a_{\\color{black}20} = {\\color{blue}-112}\n$$\n\nTo find $d$, we apply the formula with $n=20$ and $m=12$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{n} - a_{m}}{n - m} \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{20} - a_{12}}{20 - 12} \\\\\n&= (-112 - (-56))/(20 - 12) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-112 + 56}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-56}{8} \\\\\n&=-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-7$.\n\nIndeed, our result is negative, so we can be fairly confident that it is correct." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078185 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\na_{\\color{black}12} = {\\color{red}-56}, \\quad a_{\\color{black}20} = {\\color{blue}-112}.\n$$\n\nTo find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=20$ and $m=12\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{20}-{a}_{12}}{20-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-112-(-56)}{20-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-112+56}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{-56}{8} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-7.$ \n\nIndeed, our result is negative, so we can be fairly confident that it is correct." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078186 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of the arithmetic sequence that has a $7$th term equal $-34$ and a common difference of $-5$." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078186 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of the arithmetic sequence that has a $7$th term equal to $-34$ and a common difference of $-5.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078186 1074 "First, notice that $a_{7}=-34$ is $6$ jumps after $a_1$.\n\n![](images/d3/d30def27167c595e1ffa7da50b20da7f062b60b5cb81a6ff05127adadb7155a9.png)\n\nHence, we can write\n\n$$\na_{7} = a_1 + 6d\n$$\n\nand solve for $a_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{7} &= a_{1} + 6d \\\\\n- 34 &= a_{1} + 6(-5) \\\\\n- 34 &= a_{1} - 30 \\\\\n- 4 &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&=-4 + (n - 1)(-5) \\\\\n&=-4 - 5n + 5 \\\\\n&= 1 - 5n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078186 1074 "First, notice that $a_{7}=-34$ is $6$ jumps after $a_1$.\n\n![](images/ea/ea803977d4861ad654bd29ff15f706fdd1d11f71dbf7c885f175fe8168f21b66.png)\n\nHence, we can write\n\n$$\na_{7} = a_1 + 6d,\n$$\n\nand solve for $a_1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{7} & ={a}_{1}+6d \\\\ -34 & ={a}_{1}+6(-5) \\\\ -34 & ={a}_{1}-30 \\\\ -4 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-4+(n-1)(-5) \\\\  & =-4-5n+5 \\\\  & =1-5n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078194 1071 "What is the common difference of the following arithmetic sequence?\n\n$$\n7,\\: 2,\\: -3,\\: -8,\\: \\ldots\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078194 1071 "What is the common difference of the following arithmetic sequence?\n$7,\\: 2,\\: -3,\\: -8,\\: \\ldots$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078194 1074 "The common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence is the difference between any two consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common difference for our sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{2} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 2 - 7 \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078194 1074 "The common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence is the difference between any two consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common difference for our sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =2-7 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078256 1071 "Given the triangle below, find the length of $\\overline{MN}$ rounded to one decimal place.\n\n![](images/db/db0c3be4d3f18a55bfbd763e4017bdabd6c27ba7b498e21909baa4aea3d44337.png)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078256 1071 "Given the triangle below, find the length of $\\overline{MN}$ rounded to one decimal place. \n\n![](images/db/db0c3be4d3f18a55bfbd763e4017bdabd6c27ba7b498e21909baa4aea3d44337.png)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078256 1074 "For an angle $\\theta$ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overline{NO}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overline{MN}$ adjacent to the given angle $\\angle{N}.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠N) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({25}^{∘}) & =\\frac{MN}{14} \\\\ MN & =14\\operatorname{cos}⁡({25}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MN & =14\\operatorname{cos}⁡({25}^{∘}) \\\\  & =14(0.906\\,30…) \\\\  & =12.688\\,30… \\\\  & ≈12.7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078256 1074 "For an angle $\\theta$ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n- $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n- $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overline{NO}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overline{MN}$ adjacent to the given angle $\\angle{N}$.\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (m∠N) &= \\frac{adjacent leg}{hypotenuse} \\\\\n\\cos (25^{∘}) &= \\frac{MN}{14} \\\\\nMN &= 14\\cos (25^{∘})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMN &= 14\\cos (25^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 14(0.90630…) \\\\\n&= 12.68830… \\\\\n&\\approx 12.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078257 1074 "For an angle $\\theta$ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, notice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overline{MO}$ opposite the given angle $\\angle{N}$ and wish to find the length of the hypotenuse $\\overline{NO}.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the hypotenuse is *sine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(m∠N) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({32}^{∘}) & =\\frac{20}{NO} \\\\ NO\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({32}^{∘}) & =20 \\\\ NO & =\\frac{20}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({32}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}NO & =\\frac{20}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({32}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{(0.529\\,91…)} \\\\  & =37.741\\,59… \\\\  & ≈37.7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078257 1074 "For an angle $\\theta$ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n- $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}}$\n- $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite leg}}{\\textrm{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, notice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overline{MO}$ opposite the given angle $\\angle{N}$ and wish to find the length of the hypotenuse $\\overline{NO}$.\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the hypotenuse is *sine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (m∠N) &= \\frac{opposite leg}{hypotenuse} \\\\\n\\sin (32^{∘}) &= \\frac{20}{NO} \\\\\nNO \\cdot \\sin (32^{∘}) &= 20 \\\\\nNO &= (20)/(\\sin (32^{∘}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nNO &= (20)/(\\sin (32^{∘})) \\\\\n&= (20)/((0.52991…)) \\\\\n&= 37.74159… \\\\\n&\\approx 37.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078258 1071 "Given the triangle below, find the length of $\\overline{BC}$ in terms of $\\theta$.\n\n![](images/8b/8b1f3d7e1d87c7021970f45279bb404b4e9998a9d351d1e95616dac98f80d45d.png)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078258 1071 "Given the triangle below, find the length of $\\overline{BC}$ in terms of $\\theta.$\n\n![](images/8b/8b1f3d7e1d87c7021970f45279bb404b4e9998a9d351d1e95616dac98f80d45d.png)" 13194