$a_{n} =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, n \\ge 1$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078778 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $3$ and the second term is $9.$\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,n\\ge 1$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078778 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe are given the first term ${a}_{1}=3.$ Also, the common difference of the sequence can be found as\n\n$$\nd=9-3=6.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =3+(n-1)(6) \\\\  & =3+6n-6 \\\\  & =6n-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078883 1071 "Which of the three sequences below are arithmetic sequences?\n\n1. $6, 10, 14, 18,..$.\n2. $4, 8, 16, 32,..$.\n3. $87, 74, 61, 46,..$." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078883 1071 "Which of the three sequences below are arithmetic sequences?\n\nI. $6,10,14,18,...$\n\nII. $4,8,16,32,...$\n\nIII. $87,74,61,46,...$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078883 1074 "To determine whether each sequence is an arithmetic sequence, we need to look at the jumps between terms in the sequence. If the jumps are the same, then they indeed represent a common difference of an arithmetic sequence.\n\n- In sequence I, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence I is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=4.$\n\n![](images/bb/bbb6555443ed4fdf7f29da4571d421b833fb21e2556a440d821bbd4e4b986923.png)\n\n- In sequence II, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence II is not an arithmetic sequence.\n\n![](images/64/6408dd510dd59615c69c063897a6ff483c20bc485d73164e74c2f57dacc4dce8.png)\n\n- In sequence III, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence III is not an arithmetic sequence.\n\n![](images/24/246210488611eed52df8d3e777e296bff30889c7dd8122d082c5d4a6bb6d7ad6.png)\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence I is an arithmetic sequence." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078963 1071 "![](images/d9/d9e89a9b811bdc6b766b01eecf817df48968e3ea604b89bc55bc9660331194e0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n1. $lim_(x → - 1)f(x) = 4$\n2. $lim_(x → 1)f(x) = DNE$\n3. $lim_(x → 3)f(x) = 1$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186078963 1071 "![](images/d9/d9e89a9b811bdc6b766b01eecf817df48968e3ea604b89bc55bc9660331194e0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=4$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186078963 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Both limits ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $(0,2].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079115 1071 "![](images/32/32938bfdcb2ed9130304722b408b304802062b701bb35c68d7d3333c7cfe8ee6.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $AB^(―)$ rounded to one decimal place.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186079115 1071 "![](images/32/32938bfdcb2ed9130304722b408b304802062b701bb35c68d7d3333c7cfe8ee6.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ rounded to one decimal place." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079115 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ opposite the given angle $∠C.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the hypotenuse is *sine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{15} \\\\ AB & =15\\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =15\\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}) \\\\  & =15(0.788\\,01…) \\\\  & =11.820\\,16… \\\\  & ≈11.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079505 1061 472359 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186079505 1061 653769 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079506 1061 472360 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186079506 1061 653770 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079507 1061 472364 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186079507 1061 653774 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079508 1061 472367 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186079508 1061 653778 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079624 1064 "When simplifying expressions, we prefer not to leave a radical in the denominator. To eliminate a radical in an expression's denominator, we can use a process known as **rationalizing the denominator**.\n\nTo rationalize the denominator, we need to multiply both the numerator and denominator by the radical that we wish to remove from the denominator.\n\nFor instance, to rationalize the denominator in the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nall we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{x}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}} &=  \\\\\n(\\sqrt{x})/((\\sqrt{x})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the final expression no longer has a radical in the denominator. Thus, the denominator has been rationalized.\n\n**Note:** Because we multiplied the numerator and denominator by the same amount, the resulting expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}}{x}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the original fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nThey are equivalent, even though they are written differently." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186079624 1064 "When simplifying expressions, we prefer not to leave a radical in the denominator. To eliminate a radical in an expression's denominator, we can use a process known as **rationalizing the denominator**.\n\nTo rationalize the denominator, we need to multiply both the numerator and denominator by the radical that we wish to remove from the denominator.\n\nFor instance, to rationalize the denominator in the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nall we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{x}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the final expression no longer has a radical in the denominator. Thus, the denominator has been rationalized.\n\n**Note:** Because we multiplied the numerator and denominator by the same amount, the resulting expression $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}}{x}$ is equivalent to the original fraction $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ They are equivalent, even though they are written differently." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079639 1064 "Trigonometric ratios can be used to solve for a missing side of a right triangle if one side is known along with the measure of an acute angle. However, the question of which ratio to use depends on what side is given and what side is unknown.\n\n![](images/8b/8b123fed4a9a42ceb69f3a6ff68ed89f27ae42294137f0cd12a9232d329d77b0.png)\n\nIn the triangle above, we are given an angle\n\n$$\n\\theta = 42^\\circ\n$$\n\nand the length of the hypotenuse is given but the length of the side opposite is unknown.\n\nSo, we need to choose the trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite side to the hypotenuse. This is sine, and now we can just set up the ratio, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 42^{∘} &= \\frac{opposite}{hypotenuse} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we simply solve for the unknown value $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 42^{∘} &= \\frac{x}{8} \\\\\nx &= 8\\sin 42^{∘} \\\\\n&= 8(0.66913...) \\\\\n&= 5.35304... \\\\\n&\\approx 5.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nNote that we used a calculator to evaluate $\\sin(42^\\circ)$." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186079639 1064 "Trigonometric ratios can be used to solve for a missing side of a right triangle if one side is known along with the measure of an acute angle. However, the question of which ratio to use depends on what side is given and what side is unknown. \n\n![](images/8b/8b123fed4a9a42ceb69f3a6ff68ed89f27ae42294137f0cd12a9232d329d77b0.png)\n\nIn the triangle above, we are given an angle $\\theta = 42^\\circ,$ and the length of the hypotenuse is given but the length of the side opposite is unknown.\n\nSo, we need to choose the trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite side to the hypotenuse. This is sine, and now we can just set up the ratio, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{42}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we simply solve for the unknown value $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{42}^{∘} & =\\frac{x}{8} \\\\ x & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡{42}^{∘} \\\\  & =8(0.669\\,13...) \\\\  & =5.353\\,04... \\\\  & ≈5.4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nNote that we used a calculator to evaluate $\\sin(42^\\circ).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079726 1064 "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\n1,\\quad 3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 9,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that the difference (or \"jump\") between two consecutive numbers is always the same. \n\n![](images/5a/5a4433d95492b9895793bc497fce887e3a39986a4f0c686ebe8a5d9b54d4f3dc.png)\n\nA sequence where the jump never changes is called an **arithmetic sequence.** \n\nWe call this jump the **common difference**, and represent it using the letter $d.$ In this particular case, the common difference is $d=2.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079726 1064 "Consider the following sequence:\n$1,\\quad 3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 9,\\quad \\ldots$\nNotice that the difference (or \"jump\") between two consecutive numbers is always the same.\n\n![](images/5a/5a4433d95492b9895793bc497fce887e3a39986a4f0c686ebe8a5d9b54d4f3dc.png)\n\nA sequence where the jump never changes is called an **arithmetic sequence.**\n\nWe call this jump the **common difference**, and represent it using the letter $d$. In this particular case, the common difference is $d=2$." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186079727 1064 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term and $d$ is a common difference.\n\nTo illustrate how this works, suppose we want to find the $20$th term of the following arithmetic sequence:\n${\\color{blue}2}, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 11, \\: 14, \\: \\ldots$\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\nd=5-2={\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 2 + (n - 1)(3) \\\\\n&= 2 + 3n - 3 \\\\\n&= 3n - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $20$th term, we can use this formula with $n=20$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 3n - 1 \\\\\na_{20} &= 3(20) - 1 \\\\\n&= 60 - 1 \\\\\n&= 59\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $20$th term is $a_{20}=59$. Using the formula was much quicker than writing out all $20$ terms of the sequence!" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186079727 1064 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d,\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term and $d$ is a common difference.\n\nTo illustrate how this works, suppose we want to find the $20$th term of the following arithmetic sequence:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 11, \\: 14, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nThe first term is $a_1={\\color{blue}2},$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=5-2={\\color{red}3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =2+(n-1)(3) \\\\  & =2+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $20$th term, we can use this formula with $n=20\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3n-1 \\\\ {a}_{20} & =3(20)-1 \\\\  & =60-1 \\\\  & =59\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $20$th term is $a_{20}=59.$ Using the formula was much quicker than writing out all $20$ terms of the sequence!" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079728 1064 "Suppose that the fourth term of an arithmetic sequence is $6$ and the fifth term is $12$. Can we use this information to compute the common difference of the sequence?\n\nThe first thing to note is that the sequence is *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be positive.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/6b/6b82ba58c986aa32635e2ebbd8b1e0fd0affdbc81e35a015960777fa72d5f72b.png)\n\nBecause $a_4 = 6$ and $a_5 = 12$ are **consecutive terms**, meaning that $a_5$ comes straight after $a_4$ in the sequence, we can easily compute the common difference $d$ by subtracting $a_4$ from $a_5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{5} - a_{4} \\\\\n&= 12 - 6 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186079728 1064 "Suppose that the fourth term of an arithmetic sequence is $6$ and the fifth term is $12.$ Can we use this information to compute the common difference of the sequence?\n\nThe first thing to note is that the sequence is *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be positive.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/6b/6b82ba58c986aa32635e2ebbd8b1e0fd0affdbc81e35a015960777fa72d5f72b.png)\n\nBecause $a_4 = 6$ and $a_5 = 12$ are **consecutive terms**, meaning that $a_5$ comes straight after $a_4$ in the sequence, we can easily compute the common difference $d$ by subtracting $a_4$ from $a_5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{5}-{a}_{4} \\\\  & =12-6 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186079729 1064 "Suppose that we're given the second and fifth terms only of an arithmetic sequence. For example, suppose that we know that\n\n$$\na_2 = {\\color{red}8}, \\quad a_5 = {\\color{red}23}\n$$\n\nThese terms are not consecutive, and so how can we figure out the common difference $d$?\n\nFirst, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/ff/ff83c75935acca7ddd5c421cd81fad85685c68fd782d1d832c068237c751753d.png)\n\nWe see that between ${\\color{red}8}$ and ${\\color{red}23}$, there are a total of ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps. So, to find the value of a single jump, we can take the total difference and divide it by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{{\\color{red}23}\\,-\\,{\\color{red}8}}{\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{15}{3} = 5\n$$\n\nWe can check that our result $d=5$ is correct by using it to figure out the intermediate terms. Indeed, the common difference $d=5$ gets us from\n\n$$\na_2={\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nto\n\n$$\na_5={\\color{red}23}\n$$\n\nin ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps:\n\n![](images/7a/7aa192675aeb3c7be78617035b74362b38974e8501d95c0692238811ae4769f1.png)\n\nIn general, if we're given two terms $a_n$ and $a_m$ of an arithmetic sequence, then we can calculate the common difference using the following formula:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{a_n-a_m}{n-m}\n$$\n\nUsing this formula to calculate the common difference between $a_{2}=8$ and $a_{5}=23$, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{5} - a_{2}}{5 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{23 - 8}{5 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{3} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186079729 1064 "Suppose that we're given the second and fifth terms only of an arithmetic sequence. For example, suppose that we know that\n\n$$\na_2 = {\\color{red}8}, \\quad a_5 = {\\color{red}23}\n$$\n\nThese terms are not consecutive, and so how can we figure out the common difference $d?$\n\nFirst, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/ff/ff83c75935acca7ddd5c421cd81fad85685c68fd782d1d832c068237c751753d.png)\n\nWe see that between ${\\color{red}8}$ and ${\\color{red}23}$, there are a total of ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps. So, to find the value of a single jump, we can take the total difference and divide it by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{{\\color{red}23}\\,-\\,{\\color{red}8}}{\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{15}{3} = 5\n$$\n\nWe can check that our result $d=5$ is correct by using it to figure out the intermediate terms. Indeed, the common difference $d=5$ gets us from $a_2={\\color{red}8}$ to $a_5={\\color{red}23}$ in ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps:\n\n![](images/7a/7aa192675aeb3c7be78617035b74362b38974e8501d95c0692238811ae4769f1.png)\n\nIn general, if we're given two terms $a_n$ and $a_m$ of an arithmetic sequence, then we can calculate the common difference using the following formula:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{a_n-a_m}{n-m}\n$$\n\nUsing this formula to calculate the common difference between $a_{2}=8$ and $a_{5}=23,$ we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{5}-{a}_{2}}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{23-8}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186080975 1078 #edb/ref 17592186080980 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186082983 1074 "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs I, II, and IV do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, they are **not** odd functions.\n\n- On the other hand, graph III does have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graph $180^\\circ$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that graph III shows an odd function.\n\n![](images/9e/9eb4d72e1fbcc0fdefbca6301127be3bb98c6a7a8bc1b4bffb66144fe9c15010.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186082983 1074 "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs I, II, and IV do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, they are **not** odd functions.\n- On the other hand, graph III does have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graph $180^\\circ$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that graph III shows an odd function.\n\n![](images/9e/9eb4d72e1fbcc0fdefbca6301127be3bb98c6a7a8bc1b4bffb66144fe9c15010.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186082984 1071 "The table below defines the function $g(x)$. Given that $g(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b$.\n\n| **$x$** | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(x)$ | $20$ | $11$ | $-4$ | $a-3$ | $11$ | $b+5$ |" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186082984 1071 "The table below defines the function $g(x).$ Given that $g(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b.$\n\n| **$x$** | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $20$ | $11$ | $-4$ | $a-3$ | $11$ | $b+5$ |" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186082984 1074 "Since $g(x)$ is an even function, we must have that $g(-x)=g(x)$.\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\ng(-1) = g(1)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(-1) &= g(1) \\\\\n- 4 &= a - 3 \\\\\na &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\ng(-5) = g(5)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(-5) &= g(5) \\\\\n20 &= b + 5 \\\\\nb &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b = -1 + 15 = 14\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186082984 1074 "Since $g(x)$ is an even function, we must have that $g(-x)=g(x).$ \n\nUsing the fact that $g(-1) = g(1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =g(1) \\\\ -4 & =a-3 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $g(-5) = g(5),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-5) & =g(5) \\\\ 20 & =b+5 \\\\ b & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b = -1 + 15 = 14.\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186082992 1074 "Recall that the graph of an even function must be symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n- Graphs II, III, and IV are not symmetric about the $y$-axis. So, they are **not** even functions.\n\n- On the other hand, graph I is symmetric about the $y$-axis. That is to say, if we reflect graph I over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that graph I is an even function.\n\n![](images/b2/b2598584b088e924dc518aa39d4adb1966f5670c827389c90bbf089dc50ee5e8.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186082992 1074 "Recall that the graph of an even function must be symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n- Graphs II, III, and IV are not symmetric about the $y$-axis. So, they are **not** even functions.\n- On the other hand, graph I is symmetric about the $y$-axis. That is to say, if we reflect graph I over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that graph I is an even function.\n\n![](images/b2/b2598584b088e924dc518aa39d4adb1966f5670c827389c90bbf089dc50ee5e8.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186082993 1071 "Is the function $f(x)= \\mid x \\mid -1$ even, odd, both, or neither?" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186082993 1071 "Is the function $f(x)=|x| -1$ even, odd, both, or neither?" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186082993 1074 "Remember that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $x$, while\n- a function is odd if $f(-x) = -f(x)$ for all $x$.\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= \\begin{vmatrix}(-x) & -1 \\\\ = & x\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\\\\n&= f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nthe function is even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-f(x) &= - (\\begin{vmatrix}x & -1) \\\\ = - & x\\end{vmatrix}+ 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(-x) \\neq -f(x)\n$$\n\nthe function is not odd.\n\nTherefore, the function is only even." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186082993 1074 "Remember that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $x,$ while\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x) = -f(x)$ for all $x.$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =|(-x)|-1 \\\\  & =|x|-1 \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x) = f(x),$ the function is even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(|x|-1) \\\\  & =-|x|+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x) \\neq -f(x),$ the function is not odd.\n\nTherefore, the function is only even." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519549 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519569 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519604 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519544 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519555 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519556 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519580 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083185 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519538 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083185 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519539 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519587 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519591 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519528 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519585 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083420 1050 480.0 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083674 1074 "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graph IV does not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, it is **not** an odd function.\n\n- On the other hand, graphs I, II, and III do have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that graphs I, II, and III show odd functions.\n\n![](images/22/22c2e88d2e50c722c272e1a10902c58387f0ef00146be4838791c3258badaa5b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III only.\"" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186084422 1064 "A function is said to be an **even function** if it is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\nFor example, the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is depicted below, is an example of an even function. It is symmetric about the $y$-axis, meaning that if we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture.\n\n![](images/cf/cfca345c7ca094cacdbc6d7141a09e34fd116c677629a6a4c1917fb0db1f753f.png)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186084422 1064 "A function is said to be an **even function** if it is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\nFor example, the function \n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2,\n$$\n\nwhose graph is depicted below, is an example of an even function. It is symmetric about the $y$-axis, meaning that if we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture.\n\n![](images/cf/cfca345c7ca094cacdbc6d7141a09e34fd116c677629a6a4c1917fb0db1f753f.png)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186084423 1064 "Recall that the graph of the even function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2\n$$\n\nis symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/07/07d0699c46b3cbd215c461d5df3629d86cd31a93228d6790fdc65b383e8556fc.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= 1 = f(1) \\\\\nf(-2) &= 4 = f(2) \\\\\nf(-3) &= 9 = f(3) \\\\\n&=⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for every $x$ in the domain, both $x$ and $-x$ give the same value of the function. Algebraically, this fact can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $f(x)$ is said to be **even** if\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nfor each $x$ in the domain of the function." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186084423 1064 "Recall that the graph of the even function $f(x) = x^2$ is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/07/07d0699c46b3cbd215c461d5df3629d86cd31a93228d6790fdc65b383e8556fc.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =1=f(1) \\\\ f(-2) & =4=f(2) \\\\ f(-3) & =9=f(3) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for every $x$ in the domain, both $x$ and $-x$ give the same value of the function. Algebraically, this fact can be written as follows: $f(-x) = f(x).$\n\nIn general, a function $f(x)$ is said to be **even** if\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x)\n$$\n\n \nfor each $x$ in the domain of the function." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186084430 1064 "A function is said to be an **odd function** if it has **rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin**. That is to say, if we rotate the graph of an odd function by $180^\\circ$ about the origin, we end up with the same picture.\n\nFor example, the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=x,\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below, is an example of an odd function. It has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, meaning that if we rotate the graph of an odd function by $180^\\circ$ about the origin, we end up with the same picture.\n\n![](images/ff/ff49c826d9a6a36b35edf73689d30e5d9dadc976b5f1caf47c1dd0383f9893bc.png)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186084430 1064 "A function is said to be an **odd function** if it has **rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin**. That is to say, if we rotate the graph of an odd function by $180^\\circ$ about the origin, we end up with the same picture.\n\nFor example, the function\n$g(x)=x$,\nwhose graph is shown below, is an example of an odd function. It has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, meaning that if we rotate the graph of an odd function by $180^\\circ$ about the origin, we end up with the same picture.\n\n![](images/ff/ff49c826d9a6a36b35edf73689d30e5d9dadc976b5f1caf47c1dd0383f9893bc.png)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186084431 1064 "Now, recall that the graph of the odd function $g(x)=x$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n![](images/a0/a0dd6a5cbc09182a4a87569879a97da697a21eeda869ae20f512dc86a911b75e.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= -1 =-f(1) \\\\\nf(-2) &= -2 =-f(2) \\\\\nf(-3) &= -3 =-f(3) \\\\\n&=⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for every $x$ in the domain, $x$ and $-x$ give opposite values of the function. Algebraically, this fact can be written as\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x)\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $f(x)$ is said to be **odd** if\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x)\n$$\n\nfor each $x$ in the domain of the function." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186084431 1064 "Now, recall that the graph of the odd function $g(x)=x$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n![](images/a0/a0dd6a5cbc09182a4a87569879a97da697a21eeda869ae20f512dc86a911b75e.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-1=-f(1) \\\\ f(-2) & =-2=-f(2) \\\\ f(-3) & =-3=-f(3) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for every $x$ in the domain, $x$ and $-x$ give opposite values of the function. Algebraically, this fact can be written as $f(-x) = -f(x).$\n\nIn general, a function $f(x)$ is said to be **odd** if\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x)\n$$\n\n \nfor each $x$ in the domain of the function." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186119938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519551 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186119942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519532 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186119942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519542 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186124828 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(-1,4).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186124828 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(-1,4).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186133886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519559 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186161678 1071 "![](images/8f/8f5bbd64a61818dc2dd076246d9324f12d7b1123daee08f15061519a303d12d3.svg)\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the conditional probability density function $f_{Y \\,|\\, X}(y \\,|\\, x)$ and the marginal density function $f_X(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for $0 \\leq        x \\leq        1, \\: 0 \\leq        y \\leq        1.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186161678 1071 "${(3(x^{2} + y^{2}))/(3x^{2} + 1),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$, for which the conditional probability density function $f_{Y \\,\\mid \\, X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x)$ and the marginal density function $f_X(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1$." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186161678 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{Y \\,\\mid \\, X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_X (x)}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y$, and\n- $f_X (x)$ is the marginal density function for $X$.\n\nFrom the equation above, it follows that\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = f_{Y \\,\\mid \\, X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x) \\cdot f_X (x)\n$$\n\nNow, for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x) \\cdot f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= (3(x^{2} + y^{2}))/(3x^{2} + 1) \\cdot \\frac{3x^{2} + 1}{2} \\\\\n&= (3(x^{2} + y^{2}))/(2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y$, the function $f_{Y \\,\\mid \\, X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x)$ equals $0$. As a result, the product will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the joint probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{3 (x 2 + y 2) 2,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186161678 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{Y \\,|\\, X}(y \\,|\\, x) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_X (x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- $f_X (x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nFrom the equation above, it follows that\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = f_{Y \\,|\\, X}(y \\,|\\, x) \\cdot f_X (x).\n$$\n\nNow, for $0 \\leq x \\leq  1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq  1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{3{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{3{x}^{2}+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f_{Y \\,|\\, X}(y \\,|\\, x)$ equals $0.$ As a result, the product will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the joint probability density function is the following:\n![](images/e5/e50432a004c16271fe9ffa680412a4729c9d03ce85c28d821d6b03585d2e24ba.svg)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186161679 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n![](images/93/933179a6f35cb7362a1c1fea2c6b945796ebb8a436095552ade7b4d769690654.svg) \n\nFind $P \\left(Y <  \\dfrac{1}{2} \\,\\bigg|\\, X=1\\right).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186161679 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random 