vative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5(1-{x}^{2})}+3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+{x}^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186168092 1064 "The derivatives of the inverse reciprocal hyperbolic functions are shown below:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{d}{dx}(arcsch x) &= -\\frac{1}{\\mid x \\mid \\sqrt{1 + x^{2}}}, & x ∈ (- ∞, 0)∪(0, ∞) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(arsech x)|=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1 - x^{2}}}, & x ∈ (0, 1) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(arcoth x)|= \\frac{1}{1 - x^{2}}, & x ∈ (- ∞,-1)∪(1, ∞)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the derivative of the base inverse reciprocal hyperbolic functions, we can differentiate combinations of these functions, by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of\n\n$$\ny = \\arsech{(-x^2)}\n$$\n\nUsing the formulas above together with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(arsech (-x^{2})) \\\\\n&= - (1)/((-x^{2})\\sqrt{1 - (-x^{2})^{2}}) \\cdot (-x^{2})^{′} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{-x^{2}\\sqrt{1 - x^{4}}} \\cdot (-2x) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{1 - x^{4}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186168092 1064 "The derivatives of the inverse reciprocal hyperbolic functions are shown below:\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x) & =-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x) & =-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}, &  & x\\in (0,1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the derivative of the base inverse reciprocal hyperbolic functions, we can differentiate combinations of these functions, by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of\n\n$$\ny = \\arsech{(-x^2)}\n$$\n\nUsing the formulas above together with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(-{x}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1-{(-{x}^{2})}^{2}}}\\cdot {(-{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{4}}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186168093 1064 "Recall the following derivatives of the inverse hyperbolic functions:\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}, &  & \\,x\\in (1,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x) & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we arrive at the following basic integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (1,\\infty ) \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use these formulas for finding integrals. For example, let's find the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(\\dfrac{5}{2\\sqrt{x^2-1}} + 2x \\right) \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of integration and the formulas above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+2x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186168093 1064 "Recall the following derivatives of the inverse hyperbolic functions:\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\text{\\arsinh}x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\text{\\arcosh}x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}, &  & \\,x\\in (1,\\infty ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\text{\\artanh}x) & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}, &  & \\,x\\in (-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we arrive at the following basic integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\text{\\arsinh}x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\text{\\arcosh}x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (1,\\infty ) \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\text{\\artanh}x+C, &  & \\,x\\in (-1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use these formulas for finding integrals. For example, let's find the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(\\dfrac{5}{2\\sqrt{x^2-1}} + 2x \\right) \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of integration and the formulas above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+2x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\text{\\arcosh}x+{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186171297 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171298 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186171297 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171299 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186171297 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171300 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186171297 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171301 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186171297 1075 #edb/re