o the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186185424 1078 #edb/ref 17592186185428 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186188024 1071 "![](images/02/025ea896b8ef2253530a6392fc5b5c89c70df5083a673de14a1c6e13108068bd.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,2]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above. Find the maximum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,2]$ given that $f(2) = 4$." 13194139535582 17592186045417 false] [17592186188024 1071 "![](images/02/025ea896b8ef2253530a6392fc5b5c89c70df5083a673de14a1c6e13108068bd.png)\n\nThe function $f(x