e] [17592186519510 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519510 1077 "selection" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519510 1078 #edb/ref 17592186518993 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519510 1079 #uuid "fe3cf3ad-3ee5-5331-8b54-4ce6e5fd60bb" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519511 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519004 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519511 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519118 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519511 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519122 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519511 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519140 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519511 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519167 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519511 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519511 1077 "selection" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519511 1078 #edb/ref 17592186519118 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519511 1079 #uuid "fe76e9c1-efee-513d-8f78-b81b7c1d3e0f" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519503 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519512 1069 "q-236631" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519512 1071 "Consider the following arithmetic sequence.\n\n$$\n-13,\\,-9,\\,-5,\\,-1,\\,…\n$$\n\nWhat is the $36$th term of this sequence?\n\n${a}_{36}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519512 1072 #uuid "013f16dc-6316-5e58-8d55-c6702c89f1f0" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519512 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nFirst, we will write the formula for the $n$th term of this sequence. From the first few terms\n\n$$\n-13,\\,-9,\\,-5,\\,-1,\\,…,\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is ${a}_{1}=-13$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=-9-(-13)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-13+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =-13+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use our formula to find the $36$th term. All we have to do is substitute $n=36:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =4n-17 \\\\ {a}_{36} & =4(36)-17 \\\\  & =144-17 \\\\  & =127\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519424 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519513 1069 "q-145100" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519513 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{4}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519513 1072 #uuid "0309d911-015b-5e4c-9346-30c2d14fb0b7" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519513 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+y}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [4x+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+1}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x+y}{4})}{(\\frac{2x+1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+y}{4x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{2(2x+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519483 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519514 1069 "q-37508" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519514 1071 "Given that $3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x-2=-2+5x,$ solve for $x.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519514 1072 #uuid "03998afe-a211-5305-952a-0c2809482e65" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519514 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(x)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x-2 & =-2+5x \\\\ 3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-2 & =-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of ${x}^{2}.$ We can factor out the ${x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(3x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519444 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519515 1069 "q-145108" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519515 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y+x}{4y+2}, & \\,0<x<2,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{3}{4}).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519515 1072 #uuid "05787bc3-261f-5b82-bb66-810cc0eb28ae" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519515 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{3}{4}) & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{y}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{3}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+x)}{(4\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}(x+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3x}{2}]}_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[{x}^{2}+3x]_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}({(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+3(\\frac{3}{4})-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-3(\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\frac{9}{16}+\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519436 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519516 1069 "q-67539" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519516 1071 "![](images/1e/1e40e0ffa7880b3b3b63070f063de207dcfafbdbed5fdf02464e180feba715e2.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},3).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519516 1072 #uuid "069bdd21-6b0e-5746-9f9a-48c390ea491e" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519516 1074 "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=3$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=3$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519429 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519517 1069 "q-77890" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519517 1071 "Express $\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{\\sqrt{2-x}}\\,$ without radicals in the denominator." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519517 1072 #uuid "07c37b28-ce2e-5984-bd1b-1c0c09dcc7da" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519517 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{2-x}$ from the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2-x}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{\\sqrt{2-x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{\\sqrt{2-x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2-x}}{\\sqrt{2-x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2-x}}{(\\sqrt{2-x})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(x+2)(2-x)}}{2-x} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}{2-x} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519454 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519518 1069 "q-146341" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519518 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,3).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519518 1072 #uuid "097fc2ae-2fef-58ac-bba4-f69c8c04dea2" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519518 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,3) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 3.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,3) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 3}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(1,3)+f(2,1)+f(2,2)+f(2,3) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.2+0.15+0.1+0.1 \\\\  & =0.85.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519439 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519519 1069 "q-122318" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519519 1071 "![](images/42/42d1bf23b4ce679748fb1c1765d568b0b32128155f3d6e7770a1b894b0888ded.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{LM}$ in terms of $\\theta .$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519519 1072 #uuid "09ccf4cc-8d66-55a0-964d-b04cc4bd149d" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519519 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{MN}$ opposite the given angle $m∠L$  and wish to find the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{LM}.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the hypotenuse is *sine*. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(m∠L) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{6}{LM} \\\\ LM\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =6 \\\\ LM & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519470 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519520 1069 "q-365109" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519520 1071 "Express $\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}}{\\sqrt{x-4}}\\,$ without radicals in the denominator." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519520 1072 #uuid "0c5d26cb-9be7-50d4-8738-09ac8a11367a" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519520 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{x-4}$ from the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{x-4}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}}{\\sqrt{x-4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}}{\\sqrt{x-4}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x-4}}{\\sqrt{x-4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x+5}\\cdot \\sqrt{x-4}}{(\\sqrt{x-4})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(x+5)(x-4)}}{x-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+x-20}}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519499 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519521 1069 "q-12244" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519521 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ when $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519521 1072 #uuid "0cf33d77-f213-525d-99fb-8b9182428af7" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519521 1074 "Writing the radical as a rational exponent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{1/2}}={x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2\\,-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519475 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519522 1069 "q-144839" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519522 1071 "![](images/21/219e476902934a4272fb0c523fe0b623f27ef5ba2647fcf4658d0b262e64200e.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,5]$ given that $f(5)=3.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519522 1072 #uuid "1395e862-a7e8-537a-97de-69c4caaca685" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519522 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =3 \\\\ f(0) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-8 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{3,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519478 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519523 1069 "q-316301" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519523 1071 "![](images/bc/bcffe721c35a3c729644fb36ee9008095c46298a56109f0dfd75a3c06f3f7cae.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right).\n\nGiven that $f(2)=-3,$ what is the maximum value ${f}_{\\text{max}}$ of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]?$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519523 1072 #uuid "13ad1074-2be7-58b6-8b52-de567a736fd0" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519523 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-3 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(-2) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(4+(-2)) \\\\  & =-3-2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3,-1,-5\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519440 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519524 1069 "q-95640" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519524 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519524 1072 #uuid "18c5eb7d-5849-5737-9236-e416ee2ad73f" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519524 1074 "The derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519420 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519525 1069 "q-143637" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519525 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x+y}}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519525 1072 #uuid "1967c2eb-a9e1-5379-9ffd-3708e127b5c8" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519525 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{{e}^{x+y}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{x+y}}{2})}{(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2})} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519447 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519526 1069 "q-143512" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519526 1071 "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{101}$ be a random sample of size $n=101$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $2.5$ and $3.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519526 1072 #uuid "1e9c32a8-c5ef-5dca-8493-0a63cb017fc2" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519526 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=101,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0.7+4\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0+1.4+0.4+0.8 \\\\  & =2.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.7+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{8}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{2.6}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+2.8+1.6+6.4)-6.76 \\\\  & =4.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.6,\\frac{4.04}{101}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.6,0.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3) & ≈P(\\frac{2.5-2.6}{\\sqrt{0.04}}<Z<\\frac{3-2.6}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.5<Z<2) \\\\  & =Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3)=Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5).\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519497 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519527 1069 "q-215844" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519527 1071 "Consider the following arithmetic sequence.\n\n$$\n-22,\\,-31,\\,-40,\\,-49,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe common difference of this arithmetic sequence is{{field-1}}." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519527 1072 #uuid "201f78ad-e932-54f4-94ee-8dd6f6a1154d" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519527 1074 "The common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence is the difference between any two consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common difference for our sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-31-(-22) \\\\  & =-31+22 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519496 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519528 1069 "q-95091" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519528 1071 "A truck travels an average distance of $21\\,\\text{mi}$ with a gallon of gas. However, $15%$ of the time, a truck travels more than $26\\,\\text{mi}$ on a gallon of gas. Assuming that the distance the truck can travel with a gallon of gas is normally distributed, find, as a percent, the proportion of journeys that the truck travels less than $15\\,\\text{mi}$ on a gallon of gas. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0918\\,$ | $\\,0.0901\\,$ | $\\,0.0885\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ | $\\,0.1075\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ | $\\,0.1271\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ |" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519528 1072 #uuid "217980d1-12ff-5532-90b2-461f43899f04" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519528 1074 "Let $X$ represent the distance traveled by the truck with a gallon of gas. Then we have $X∼N(21,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X\\le 15).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>26)=0.15.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 26) & =1-P(X>26) \\\\  & =1-0.15 \\\\  & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 26) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{26-21}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{5}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)≈1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.2073 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.2073} \\\\  & ≈4.8239.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X\\le 15)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 15) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{15-21}{4.8239}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.24) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.24) \\\\  & =0.1075 \\\\  & ≈10.8%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $10.8%$ of the time, a gallon of gas travels only $15\\,\\text{mi}$ or less." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519464 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519529 1069 "q-5271" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519529 1071 "An arithmetic sequence has ${a}_{3}=18$ and ${a}_{4}=23.$ What is the common difference of the sequence?" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519529 1072 #uuid "226f3771-4340-5c2a-b0b8-27bc76c058c5" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519529 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *positive*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive, we can find the common difference by subtracting ${a}_{3}$ from ${a}_{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{4}-{a}_{3} \\\\  & =23-18 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=5.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519453 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519530 1069 "q-365110" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519530 1071 "Express $\\frac{\\sqrt{m-n}}{\\sqrt{m+n}}\\,$ without radicals in the denominator." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519530 1072 #uuid "22cd57f3-188f-5b72-9854-fd7357950a74" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519530 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{m+n}$ from the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{m+n}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{m-n}}{\\sqrt{m+n}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{m-n}}{\\sqrt{m+n}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{m+n}}{\\sqrt{m+n}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{m-n}\\cdot \\sqrt{m+n}}{(\\sqrt{m+n})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(m-n)(m+n)}}{m+n} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{{m}^{2}-{n}^{2}}}{m+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519462 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519531 1069 "q-341493" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519531 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=-3\\,\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519531 1072 #uuid "24bbcf4d-e35e-5166-86db-6c3a81f4f4ca" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519531 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[-3\\,\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x)] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x)] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}]\\cdot (2x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2x\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519484 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519532 1069 "q-101160" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519532 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{2}+3y-18}{{y}^{2}-3y}.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519532 1072 #uuid "2778d4f8-72c7-5929-98a7-5df304873786" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519532 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{2}+3y-18}{{y}^{2}-3y} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}+3(3)-18}{{3}^{2}-3(3)}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}+3y-18}{{y}^{2}-3y} & =\\frac{(y+6)(y-3)}{y(y-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y+6}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{2}+3y-18}{{y}^{2}-3y} & ={lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{y+6}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519498 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519533 1069 "q-122362" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519533 1071 "![](images/80/804c19a20b6f47707a07c7bdcee3cbd727eec2c270109e30eb55d949ace8edf0.png)\n\nCalculate the value of $BC$ to the nearest integer." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519533 1072 #uuid "2ca7cacc-1a5f-515a-8db1-0999afb66a6b" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519533 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AC}$ opposite to the given angle $∠B$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{BC}$ adjacent $∠B.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘}) & =\\frac{2.91}{BC} \\\\ BC\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘}) & =2.91 \\\\ BC & =\\frac{2.91}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\frac{2.91}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({20}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.91}{(0.363\\,97…)} \\\\  & =7.995\\,15… \\\\  & ≈8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519448 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519534 1069 "q-236649" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519534 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of the arithmetic sequence that has ${a}_{25}=95$ and the common difference $d=4.$\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,n\\ge 1$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519534 1072 #uuid "36a772ef-4e2c-580e-97bd-8718ab74d591" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519534 1074 "Since ${a}_{25}=95$ is $24$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{25} & ={a}_{1}+24d \\\\ 95 & ={a}_{1}+24(4) \\\\ 95 & ={a}_{1}+96 \\\\ -1 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-1+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =-1+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519507 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519535 1069 "q-143641" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519535 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32} & \\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519535 1072 #uuid "3b02f20a-35d0-57b1-ada9-f1273f1f3904" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519535 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{32}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{32}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{64}\\cdot (y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256}, & \\,0<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32})}{(\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}, & \\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519481 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519536 1069 "q-143613" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519536 1071 "$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3y}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519536 1072 #uuid "403a78d0-86af-59ab-a2a0-082e022aaef5" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519536 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nFrom the equation above, it follows that\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3y}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6x{e}^{-3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ equals $0.$ As a result, the product will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the joint probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6x{e}^{-3y}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519479 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519537 1069 "q-146466" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519537 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(12,6)=0.81,\\,F(12,4)=0.3,\\,F(6,6)=0.17,\\,F(6,4)=0.1,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(6<X\\le 12,4<Y\\le 6).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519537 1072 #uuid "45f60a39-7c3b-515c-9608-b1c0fb2b6cd7" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519537 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12,15\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/71/715408bbd41d8551b8eadce9df6259f763ec34a702d48f4d116c4164ae5fda50.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/5c/5c50878b85d26a87d6220a99f6fbe896925a6802801117239a5ba56dcba46906.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(12,4)$ and $(6,6).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,4).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6<X\\le 12,4<Y\\le 6) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 4)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(12,4)-F(6,6)+F(6,4) \\\\  & =0.81-0.3-0.17+0.1 \\\\  & =0.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519509 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519538 1069 "q-6695" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519538 1071 "Is the function $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+x-6$ even, odd, or neither?" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519538 1072 #uuid "49d7ac71-7387-5bcd-8351-170b2eb2bef2" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519538 1074 "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{3}+(-x)-6 \\\\  & =-3{x}^{3}-x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne f(x),$ the function is not even. \n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(3{x}^{3}+x-6) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{3}-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne -f(x)$, the function is not odd. \n\nTherefore, the function is neither even nor odd." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519490 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519539 1069 "q-6694" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519539 1071 "Is the function $f(x)=-2x-5$ even, odd, or neither?" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519539 1072 #uuid "5272ce70-1353-5fba-a94b-5e92203eee02" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519539 1074 "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =-2(-x)-5 \\\\  & =2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne f(x),$ the function is not even. \n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(-2x-5) \\\\  & =2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne -f(x)$, the function is not odd. \n\nTherefore, the function is neither even nor odd." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519455 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519540 1069 "q-111045" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519540 1071 "Rationalize $\\frac{10p}{\\sqrt{5q}}.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519540 1072 #uuid "54170d78-262c-5604-939a-66b79556fcdc" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519540 1074 "We need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{5q}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{5q},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10p}{\\sqrt{5q}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10p}{\\sqrt{5q}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5q}}{\\sqrt{5q}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10p\\cdot \\sqrt{5q}}{\\sqrt{5q}\\cdot \\sqrt{5q}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10p\\cdot \\sqrt{5q}}{5q} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2p\\cdot \\sqrt{5q}}{\\cancel{5}\\cdot q} & = \\\\ \\frac{2p\\sqrt{5q}}{q}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519488 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519541 1069 "q-146465" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519541 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(7,10)=0.82,\\,F(7,4)=0.11,\\,F(7,2)=0.05,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 7,4<Y\\le 10).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519541 1072 #uuid "560539a1-a567-51bf-978e-83db525442bc" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519541 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/9e/9e7509e29912cc54dde9a86786f1d171a49b89e7b5f7305ef025582b3402350a.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/78/78a130ca07be1acecc4655a557abb8d3d037da28f143d0696272d12898b6b4cd.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,10)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/e4/e4ff1e7cd8259fa9bcaae75210a7a3f2aeeb547ceba46b2fb411f94133372fa2.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7,4<Y\\le 10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 10)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(7,10)-F(7,4) \\\\  & =0.82-0.11 \\\\  & =0.71.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519500 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519542 1069 "q-313149" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519542 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519542 1072 #uuid "5986bf81-4caa-5e55-8c99-36ab322d7c9c" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519542 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2} & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}-5(-2)-14}{-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+10-14}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2} & =\\frac{(x-7)(x+2)}{x+2} \\\\  & =x-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}(x-7) & =(-2)-7 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519417 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519543 1069 "q-76518" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519543 1071 "Given an arithmetic sequence that has ${a}_{8}=8$ and the common difference $d=4$, what is the general formula for the $n$th term of the sequence?" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519543 1072 #uuid "59d1d85b-3b0d-5f71-98d3-9151bc5e371c" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519543 1074 "Since ${a}_{8}=8$ is $7$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & ={a}_{1}+7d \\\\ 8 & ={a}_{1}+7(4) \\\\ 8 & ={a}_{1}+28 \\\\ -20 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-20+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =-20+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n-24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519474 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519544 1069 "q-36586" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519544 1071 "The table below defines the function $f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b.$\n\n| **$x$** | $-123$ | $-71$ | $-29$ | $29$ | $71$ | $123$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $8$ | $a+12$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $20$ | $b-2$ |" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519544 1072 #uuid "5e293714-80a3-5b8a-84fe-6e44867ed437" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519544 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is an even function, we must have that $f(-x)=f(x).$ \n\nUsing the fact that $f(-71)=f(71),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-71) & =f(71) \\\\ a+12 & =20 \\\\ a & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $f(-123)=f(123),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-123) & =f(123) \\\\ 8 & =b-2 \\\\ 10 & =b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=8+10=18.\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519463 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519545 1069 "q-145101" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519545 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519545 1072 #uuid "60bd267c-251c-5e47-b502-67f097fb7ef0" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519545 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{y}{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{36}+\\frac{xy}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{36}+\\frac{3y}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{y}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y+3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y+3}{4}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12})}{(\\frac{2y+3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519493 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519546 1069 "q-335453" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519546 1071 "Which statement correctly describes a **stable** equilibrium point?" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519546 1072 #uuid "63f582f6-9d11-5d33-b64d-69b2836d097f" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519546 1074 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519435 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519547 1069 "q-250820" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519547 1071 "A triangle $△ABC$ has $m∠A={38}^{∘},$ $m∠B={90}^{∘},$ and $AC=18.$ Find $AB$ rounded to the nearest integer.\n\n$AB≈${{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519547 1072 #uuid "65457b1f-86ca-5676-81db-c0f19c1bbed5" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519547 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the given triangle.\n\n![](images/d0/d097dca887930886a231b4b3a71e3236edcc25ec50a886f197a8c3111ff064fb.png)\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠A.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{18} \\\\ AB & =18\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =18\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) \\\\  & =18(0.788\\,01…) \\\\  & =14.184\\,19… \\\\  & ≈14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519467 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519548 1069 "q-143607" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519548 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519548 1072 #uuid "6a8bad50-df40-5364-9a82-59b2638eab96" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519548 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519423 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519549 1069 "q-72963" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519549 1071 "![](images/da/da6aa84a7b37d9b776f88a7e3b6eb4cf70c303c3c0c463abd313c51bfa0781a2.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be graphs of odd functions?" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519549 1072 #uuid "6c5eea4e-4b84-5cc7-8353-9e67cf3ab163" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519549 1074 "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs I, III, and IV do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, they are **not** odd functions.\n\n- On the other hand, graph II does have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that graph II shows an odd function.\n\n![](images/34/348ca99660cff1d4db774d39b543aa7cb79cf0a1787f11046e9b8a4d72fd83e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519443 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519550 1069 "q-341457" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519550 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=3\\,\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x-1),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{|x-1|}$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519550 1072 #uuid "6edfa6ba-1072-5bf1-bdfe-2aaa6d5af318" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519550 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[3\\,\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x-1)] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x-1)] \\\\  & =3\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{1+(x-1)^{2}}}]\\cdot (x-1)^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{|x-1|\\sqrt{1+({x}^{2}-2x+1)}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|x-1|}\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2x+2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519427 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519551 1069 "q-3749" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519551 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519551 1072 #uuid "72828711-fc6d-5cc1-8d14-0b819a351e61" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519551 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{-2(1)^{4}+6(1)^{2}-4}{(1)^{2}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{-2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2({x}^{2}-2)({x}^{2}-1)}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =-2({x}^{2}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\,\\,-2({x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-2(1-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519552 1069 "q-146408" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519552 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,2,4,6\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,3\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>6,y>3.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519552 1072 #uuid "741ee6eb-9658-524e-8fcb-9b428519f04d" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519552 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,2,4,6\\}\\times \\{0,1,3\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>6$ and $y>3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/63/6340ca35646830558631c7fe523698a201c5acf26d1360910ab207c30f92e41f.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at most $6$ and $x>6,$ every possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Similarly, every value in the support of $Y$ is at most $3$ and $y>3,$ so every possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$\n\nTherefore, the region $Δ$ contains the entire joint support, and\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=1.\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519506 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519553 1069 "q-54245" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519553 1071 "A triangle $△ABC$ has $m∠A={37}^{∘},$ $m∠B={90}^{∘},$ and $AC=25.$ Find $AB$ rounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519553 1072 #uuid "75f7a455-462c-51e4-9b1a-b0d79be3c2a9" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519553 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the given triangle.\n\n![](images/70/70ac3cbbc7209f71bd8095bb725d1dbb847c827bdfdc8220c25115fb120532b4.png)\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠A.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{25} \\\\ AB & =25\\operatorname{cos}⁡({37}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =25\\operatorname{cos}⁡({37}^{∘}) \\\\  & =25(0.798\\,63…) \\\\  & =19.965\\,88… \\\\  & ≈20,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519445 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519554 1069 "q-47827" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519554 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=-100.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519554 1072 #uuid "7712c3e1-7e39-5f21-96a2-73fbade1bb33" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519554 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is constant, its derivative is zero. Therefore, ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519431 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519555 1069 "q-5891" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519555 1071 "The table below defines the function $f(x).$ Is the function even, odd, or neither?\n\n| **$x$** | $-99$ | $-52$ | $-8$ | $8$ | $52$ | $99$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $150$ | $70$ | $0$ | $-11$ | $-92$ | $-180$ |" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519555 1072 #uuid "7d2fbf06-bec2-5ef7-8e27-5ab81fcb62b5" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519555 1074 "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of $f$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of $f$\n\nFirst, we have\n\n$$\nf(-99)=150\\ne f(99).\n$$\n\nThis means that $f(-x)\\ne f(x)$ at least for some values of $x$ in the domain of the function. So, the function is not even.\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\nf(-99)=150\\ne -f(99).\n$$\n\nThis means that $f(-x)\\ne -f(x)$ at least for some values of $x$ in the domain of the function. So, the function is not odd.\n\nHence, the function is neither even nor odd." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519508 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519556 1069 "q-71844" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519556 1071 "![](images/01/019a969103cb8aca2da4e752240079e90bc1d19e47bdadedfb0a447a3a100367.png)\n\nThe figure above shows part of the graph of $y=f(x)$ which is defined on the interval $x\\in [-4,4].$ Given that $f(x)$ is even, which of the following gives the complete graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519556 1072 #uuid "7ddac438-ed03-5ef8-ae58-2b427cb03c98" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519556 1074 "Recall that the graph of an even function must be symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\nSo, we can obtain the missing part of the graph by reflecting the given part over the $y$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the complete graph of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcb80b98841bd0c5f08b5ebc8f15abae62dfbe9a4ba0f3a922ea3e482318a96a.png)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519480 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519557 1069 "q-95503" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519557 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=-3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡4x,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519557 1072 #uuid "7e70aada-1f44-5184-b986-16d8ac2cfc36" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519557 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡4x) \\\\  & =-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡4x) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{|4x|\\sqrt{1+(4x)^{2}}})\\cdot (4x)^{′} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4|x|\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519558 1069 "q-344166" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519558 1071 "![](images/31/31fd7b63abbf8224421ee53b0703e9cc72fac2f9a8ffc3e460218bb42c4f229a.png)\n\nThe function $q(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,9].$ The graph of ${q}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $q(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $12.$\n\nGiven that $q(9)=-1,$ what is the minimum value ${q}_{\\text{min}}$ of $q(x)$ on the interval $[1,9]?$\n\n${q}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519558 1072 #uuid "82901cf9-17f2-500f-9676-c95d18dab327" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519558 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $q(x)$ on $[1,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${q}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,9),$ and ${q}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $q$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(9) & =-1 \\\\ q(1) & =q(9)+{\\int }_{9}^{1}{q}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =q(9)-{\\int }_{1}^{9}{q}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1-12 \\\\  & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $q$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $q$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,-13\\}=-13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $q$ is ${q}_{\\text{min}}=-13,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $q(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=1.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519450 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519559 1069 "q-126866" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519559 1071 "![](images/0f/0f1dd3c66e3bf7d25f286720b360d6af1d6361804ab3ff52835d94f78e5084ee.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519559 1072 #uuid "881df761-fca2-5afa-aea8-d6a6e63a7521" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519559 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-3y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(-3))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({4}^{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (16-2) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519414 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519560 1069 "q-100790" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519560 1071 "Which of the three sequences below are arithmetic sequences?\n\nI. $-2,\\,-4,\\,-6,\\,-8,...$\n\nII. $-2,\\,-9,\\,-16,\\,-23,...$\n\nIII. $-2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,16,...$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519560 1072 #uuid "8dd21bc7-cc1c-556a-83c5-25089927d9b3" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519560 1074 "To determine whether each sequence is an arithmetic sequence, we need to look at the jumps between terms in the sequence. If the jumps are the same, then they indeed represent a common difference of an arithmetic sequence. \n\n- In sequence I, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence I is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-2.$\n\n![](images/c5/c599604e6630b0dbf74d646f93b6330b5d310f35bfbce7b008f16d1686534c77.png)\n\n- In sequence II, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence II is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-7.$\n\n![](images/b3/b35cc27bfa6f7fe2171529a0c84e9ab8d33c122e932512fae1b06fe21cc9bc94.png)\n\n- In sequence III, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence III is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=6.$\n\n![](images/9a/9aa43107c2b5c975450590f4bc126273b45ddc72f1acfd3d35eadff0313ded1b.png)\n\nIn conclusion, sequences I, II, and III are arithmetic sequences." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519428 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519561 1069 "q-276897" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519561 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of the following expression:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"40\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\">$\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{pq}}\\,=\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"40\">{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"40\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"40\">$pq$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519561 1072 #uuid "8dffe1e2-d854-56ac-895d-ab0f043c857c" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519561 1074 "First, we apply the quotient rule and put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{pq}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{pq}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{pq}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{pq},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{pq}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{pq}}{\\sqrt{pq}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11pq}}{(\\sqrt{pq})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11pq}}{pq}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519422 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519562 1069 "q-5284" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519562 1071 "Find the general formula  for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence that begins with \n\n$$\n8,\\,4,\\,0,\\,…\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519562 1072 #uuid "8eb6a381-be92-55fb-a425-16a442e609ca" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519562 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nFrom the first few terms\n\n$$\n8,\\,4,\\,0,\\,…,\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is $f(1)=8$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=4-8=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =8+(n-1)(-4) \\\\  & =8-4n+4 \\\\  & =12-4n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is $f(n)=12-4n.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519456 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519563 1069 "q-143619" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519563 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3x, & \\,y<x<1\\,,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519563 1072 #uuid "91a2a3a1-8ae1-5b13-976f-9bc558602e26" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519563 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$  \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{y}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $y<x<1\\,,\\,0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{(\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{1-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{1-{y}^{2}}, & \\,0<y<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}) & =P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{1}{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}\\frac{2x}{(1-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2})}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}\\frac{2x}{(\\frac{35}{36})}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}{[{x}^{2}]}_{1/4}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}(1-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519446 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519564 1069 "q-103251" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519564 1071 "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if its sixth and seventh terms are $6$ and $11$, respectively." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519564 1072 #uuid "947d1f31-89e5-5a9c-9e34-33e153098c01" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519564 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *positive*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive, we can find the common difference by subtracting ${a}_{6}$ from ${a}_{7}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{7}-{a}_{6} \\\\  & =11-6 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=5.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519442 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519565 1069 "q-146394" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519565 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(0,\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519565 1072 #uuid "9adae9aa-2d5a-51d9-80a6-fbe056bdc031" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519565 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0,\\frac{3}{2}) & =P(X\\le 0,Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 0,Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0,\\frac{3}{2}) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 0}{\\sum }_{y\\le 3/2}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(-1,-1)+f(-1,0)+f(-1,1) \\\\  & =0.15+0.25+0.05 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519415 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519566 1069 "q-5288" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519566 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $5$ and the common difference is $1.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519566 1072 #uuid "9b2690e5-910c-5f9f-9900-ab879a8e0507" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519566 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the first term is ${a}_{1}=5$ and the common difference is $d=1.$ So, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =5+(n-1)(1) \\\\  & =5+n-1 \\\\  & =4+n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the formula for the $n$th term is ${a}_{n}=4+n.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519495 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519567 1069 "q-341470" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519567 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({x}^{4}+2),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2}$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519567 1072 #uuid "a0460884-e620-5591-b0cf-266181de3168" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519567 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({x}^{4}+2)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{|{x}^{4}+2|\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{4}+2)}^{2}}}]\\cdot {({x}^{4}+2)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{({x}^{4}+2)\\sqrt{1+({x}^{8}+4{x}^{4}+4)}}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2}\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{8}+4{x}^{4}+5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519504 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519568 1069 "q-143620" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519568 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{5}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{4}{3}).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519568 1072 #uuid "a05be7fd-94f3-5604-ad03-bc6e74737579" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519568 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{5}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{4}{3}) & ={\\int }_{1/5}^{1}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{4}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/5}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1/5}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519461 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519569 1069 "q-110645" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519569 1071 "![](images/67/67e6621b71176612aa5b515830cf4ce26fc29d99345269383235ca9f3a7f0a32.png)\n\nThe figure above shows part of the graph of $y=f(x)$ which is defined on the interval $x\\in [-6,6].$ Given that $f(x)$ is odd, which of the following gives the complete graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519569 1072 #uuid "a10e83cb-86c5-591c-aa72-4d43e4109d66" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519569 1074 "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\nSo, we can obtain the missing part of the graph by rotating the given part ${180}^{∘}$ over the origin. \n\nTherefore, the complete graph of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/08/0884ca2bc46d183511c4777c70d3f767ca6d5bad3c08b17ca939cf6382fd45ee.png)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519437 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519570 1069 "q-276847" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519570 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of the following expression:\n\n| ${p}^{2}\\sqrt{36}$ | __ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2\\sqrt{3s}$ | __ | $s$ |" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519570 1072 #uuid "a218b702-260e-5626-9b2a-10fbf0615a24" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519570 1074 "We must remove the radical $\\sqrt{3s}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{3s},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{2}\\sqrt{36}}{2\\sqrt{3s}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{p}^{2}\\sqrt{36}}{2\\sqrt{3s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3s}}{\\sqrt{3s}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6{p}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3s}}{2\\sqrt{3s}\\cdot \\sqrt{3s}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6{p}^{2}\\sqrt{3s}}{2{(\\sqrt{3s})}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6{p}^{2}\\sqrt{3s}}{2\\cdot 3s} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{6}{p}^{2}\\sqrt{3s}}{\\cancel{6}s} & = \\\\ \\frac{{p}^{2}\\sqrt{3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519416 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519571 1069 "q-146411" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519571 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<2,y<1.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519571 1072 #uuid "a2622f87-4a36-506a-86f1-c353a2d6ebaa" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519571 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\times \\{1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y<1,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/39/3911ef3923eabce680eb406a93c02aaacd2b91f829294eb7ac0e26a11753a703.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $1$ and $y<1,$ no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$ Therefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519421 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519572 1069 "q-344108" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519572 1071 "![](images/84/84deebb7aa20aee2bb0b9e57db825fbdfac1482ca4aec7202ba5458c5603492f.png)\n\nThe function $t(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${t}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $t(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$\n\nGiven that $t(2)=3,$ what is the maximum value ${t}_{\\text{max}}$ of $t(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]?$\n\n${t}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519572 1072 #uuid "a322e8e4-fc5c-530f-aece-0d3557ac2a42" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519572 1074 "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $t(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${t}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${t}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $t$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(2) & =3 \\\\ t(-4) & =t(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{t}^{′}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =t(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{t}^{′}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3-(-8) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $t$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $t$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{t}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $t$ is ${t}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $t(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-4.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519430 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519573 1069 "q-344182" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519573 1071 "![](images/04/04e4299da4d4c29cce751d04ee6ee29380a3dead3ab3456f6645858b5d9d106b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(3)=2,$ what is the minimum value ${f}_{\\text{min}}$ of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,3]?$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519573 1072 #uuid "a3fba9ae-fcbd-5c75-9ad3-403a4a333328" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519573 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/c3/c30d7e1d2c9eb939726d85b0ee5d51a2bdc0cfe73fab92f0ba29839a9f18be4e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-7.5 \\\\  & =-5.5 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-3+7.5) \\\\  & =2-4.5 \\\\  & =-2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,-5.5,-2.5\\}=-5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519449 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519574 1069 "q-67189" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519574 1071 "What is the common difference for the following arithmetic sequence?\n\n$$\n-5,\\,-12,\\,-19,\\,-26,\\,…\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519574 1072 #uuid "a8cf8516-d7bc-5fa0-86d7-e74e836777c8" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519574 1074 "The common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence is the difference between any two consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common difference for our sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-12-(-5) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519501 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519575 1069 "q-214179" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519575 1071 "An arithmetic sequence has ${a}_{7}=6$ and ${a}_{8}=-3.$ The common difference of this sequence is $d=${{field-1}}." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519575 1072 #uuid "a939b213-95ee-5354-bca1-280bbeed2f43" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519575 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive, we can find the common difference by subtracting ${a}_{7}$ from ${a}_{8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{8}-{a}_{7} \\\\  & =-3-6 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-9.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519489 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519576 1069 "q-102182" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519576 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\frac{1}{e}.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519576 1072 #uuid "adc818d6-3be7-557c-93b2-e7ce26f749ac" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519576 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is constant, its derivative is zero. Therefore, ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519471 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519577 1069 "q-344104" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519577 1071 "![](images/21/21e0ed364f3a04068c7f3775715432b218be2ada6b54d97ae6836f28528006da.png)\n\nThe function $s(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,9].$ The graph of ${s}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $s(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$\n\nGiven that $s(9)=-2,$ what is the maximum value ${s}_{\\text{max}}$ of $s(x)$ on the interval $[0,9]?$\n\n${s}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519577 1072 #uuid "b09a5298-e405-5905-9c2a-958d82f56eb7" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519577 1074 "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $s(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${s}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${s}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $s$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(9) & =-2 \\\\ s(0) & =s(9)+{\\int }_{9}^{0}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =s(9)-{\\int }_{0}^{9}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2-(-6) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $s$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $s$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{s}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,4\\}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $s$ is ${s}_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $s(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519441 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519578 1069 "q-143610" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519578 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5y\\sqrt{x}, & 0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x\\sqrt{x}}{2}, & 0<x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519578 1072 #uuid "b3c1aae3-e457-548d-bfd0-11222eb5ea0e" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519578 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5y\\sqrt{x}}{(\\frac{5x\\sqrt{x}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{x}, & 0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519476 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519579 1069 "q-19977" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519579 1071 "Find the third term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $23$ and the common difference is $-7.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519579 1072 #uuid "b7af1a90-ae65-5ea4-82bf-4487562443e4" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519579 1074 "We will list terms of the sequence until we get to the third term. \n\nWe start with the first term $23,$ and then we calculate each subsequent term by subtracting $7$ from the previous term:\n\n![](images/86/86fe86315966ba57e674e652510c2814ab94f0cf8869656c9fa3b7c934801b9f.png)\n\nTherefore, the third term is $9.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519426 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519580 1069 "q-110598" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519580 1071 "![](images/b0/b0189edb7d5582896a6b56623fa5b33c1dc9b65ff3d8ccaf634df452a72d9e0f.png)\n\nThe figure above shows part of the graph of $y=f(x)$ which is defined on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$ Given that $f(x)$ is even, which of the following gives the complete graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519580 1072 #uuid "bc5a7bfe-60f8-5841-b9f2-93c7cc229a66" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519580 1074 "Recall that the graph of an even function must be symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\nSo, we can obtain the missing part of the graph by reflecting the given part over the $y$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the complete graph of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/22/22914a2693ec654474ca8572587386de4c35ae3feb6663bd1c915c29bbf9e5dd.png)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519419 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519581 1069 "q-214183" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519581 1071 "Consider the following arithmetic sequence.\n\n$$\n7,\\,3,\\,-1,\\,-5,\\,…\n$$\n\nWhat is the $34$th term of this sequence?\n\n${a}_{34}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519581 1072 #uuid "c129ef1c-2ac1-51e8-b05a-e3f368cf0b6f" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519581 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nFirst, we will write the formula for the $n$th term of this sequence. From the first few terms\n\n$$\n7,\\,3,\\,-1,\\,-5,\\,…,\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is ${a}_{1}=7$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=3-7=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =7+(n-1)(-4) \\\\  & =7-4n+4 \\\\  & =11-4n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use our formula to find the $34$th term. All we have to do is substitute $n=34:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =11-4n \\\\ {a}_{34} & =11-4(34) \\\\  & =11-136 \\\\  & =-125\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519434 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519582 1069 "q-14003" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519582 1071 "Determine the real roots of the cubic equation $5{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}-60x=15x.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519582 1072 #uuid "cab62649-3f2e-56ae-bfd6-8c34dbebf0bf" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519582 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(x)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}-60x & =15x \\\\ 5{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}-75x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5x.$ We can factor out the $5x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}-75x & =0 \\\\ 5x({x}^{2}-2x-15) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${x}^{2}-2x-15$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x({x}^{2}-2x-15) & =0 \\\\ 5x(x-5)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=5,$ and $x=-3.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519418 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519583 1069 "q-277015" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519583 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of the following expression:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"80\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\">$\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{x+2}}\\,=\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"80\">{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"60\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"60\">$x+2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519583 1072 #uuid "cb60bd4d-fd24-5677-a17c-242785ce4b4c" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519583 1074 "First, we apply the quotient rule and put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{x+2}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{x+2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the radical $\\sqrt{x+2}$ from the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{x+2}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{x+2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{x+2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{\\sqrt{x+2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{x+2}}{\\sqrt{x+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{x+2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5(x+2)}}{(\\sqrt{x+2})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5(x+2)}}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519502 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519584 1069 "q-143622" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519584 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519584 1072 #uuid "d2c3b01f-8b1d-5c9b-95df-4bf4aec9fac1" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519584 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{4}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{x}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{x}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,0<y<2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4}y\\sqrt{x})}{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}) & =P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y<\\frac{3}{4}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{3}{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519432 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519585 1069 "q-133236" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519585 1071 "The average height of a player in a particular professional basketball league is $78$ inches. Additionally, $15%$ of players are over $82$ inches tall. Assuming that a player's height is normally distributed, find the percentage of players over $75$ inches tall, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ | $\\,0.2148\\,$ |" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519585 1072 #uuid "d34b6be0-737c-5c5c-907f-c713ce8e6b32" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519585 1074 "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected player. Then we have $X∼N(78,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>75).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>82)=0.15.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 82) & =1-P(X>82) \\\\  & =1-0.15 \\\\  & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 82) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{82-78}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)≈1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.2591 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.2591} \\\\  & ≈3.85951.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>75)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>75) & =1-P(X\\le 75) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{75-78}{3.85951}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le -0.78) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.78) \\\\  & =1-0.2177 \\\\  & =0.7823 \\\\  & ≈78.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $78.2%$ of players are over $75$ inch tall." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519458 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519586 1069 "q-5260" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519586 1071 "Find the fourth term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $1$ and the common difference is $-2.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519586 1072 #uuid "d4919d08-0fd6-56c9-962f-b38905295542" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519586 1074 "We will list terms of the sequence until we get to the fourth term. \n\nWe start with the first term $1,$ and then we calculate each subsequent term by subtracting $2$ from the previous term:\n\n![](images/2f/2f8e4976e5ea05c0d238959425d4d2d5ece23d4bfae844e533e9eb484f9de18a.png)\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is $-5.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519466 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519587 1069 "q-324738" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519587 1071 "The lifespan of LED light bulbs is normally distributed with a mean of $40,000$ hours and an unknown standard deviation $\\sigma .$ Tests show that $10%$ of bulbs fail before $36,000$ hours. Assuming a normal model, find the standard deviation, **rounded to the nearest integer.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,z\\,$ | $0.75$ | $0.8$ | $0.85$ | $0.9$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$h$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519587 1072 #uuid "d6b501ec-18d5-51e8-9a88-2c1c73e44bb9" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519587 1074 "Let $X$ represent the lifespan of an LED bulb. Then we have $X∼N(40,000,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<36,000)=0.10.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<36,000) & =0.10 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{36,000-40,000}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ -\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.9)=1.2816,\n$$\n\nand therefore, by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.10)=-1.2816.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & =-1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & =1.2816 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{4,000}{1.2816} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈3,121\\,\\text{h.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519473 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519588 1069 "q-341497" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519588 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=-4\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x-1),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519588 1072 #uuid "db2fa4fe-6b60-51ee-9a41-fc33e6f273b7" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519588 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-4\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x-1)] \\\\  & =-4\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{(x-1)\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}]\\cdot (x-1)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(x-1)\\sqrt{1-({x}^{2}-2x+1)}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(x-1)\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(x-1)\\sqrt{\\,x(2-x)\\,}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519468 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519589 1069 "q-34999" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519589 1071 "![](images/39/39c2c86e1fca1dbcf80435237ba31fc4aed9ed8213578b4c5f3971408da59f2d.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519589 1072 #uuid "dc22d572-0b94-5462-9d85-2c1d61a16284" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519589 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=3,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.$ Meanwhile, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\n \nHence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2.$ Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519505 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519590 1069 "q-334717" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519590 1071 "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.3$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.1$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.7$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519590 1072 #uuid "e4d56835-15e1-5dd9-b189-b250c69cae71" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519590 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.3)| & =|1-0.3+\\frac{(0.3)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{149}{200}| \\\\  & =0.745<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.1)| & =|1-1.1+\\frac{(1.1)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{101}{200}| \\\\  & =0.505<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.7)| & =|1-1.7+\\frac{(1.7)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{149}{200}| \\\\  & =0.745<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|1-2.9+\\frac{(2.9)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{461}{200}| \\\\  & =2.305>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the modified Euler method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519591 1069 "q-95082" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519591 1071 "A group of students took a test and got an average score of $79.$ Also, $15%$ of the students scored lower than $66.$ Assuming that the scores of the students are normally distributed, find the standard deviation of their scores, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519591 1072 #uuid "e64f0682-cd52-5135-b0b3-08e76e7a1873" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519591 1074 "Let $X$ represent the grade obtained by a student. Then we have $X∼N(79,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 66)=0.15.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 66) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{66-79}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{13}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364,\n$$\n\nand therefore by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(1-0.85)={Φ}^{-1}(0.15)=-1.0364.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{13}{\\sigma } & =-1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{-13}{-1.0364} \\\\  & ≈12.54.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519511 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519592 1069 "q-144941" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519592 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{10}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9}{10}, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519592 1072 #uuid "e6d9b032-eda8-55d5-b69c-37b94f5fdf98" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519592 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{10})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9}{10})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+9}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519491 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519593 1069 "q-344167" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519593 1071 "![](images/6d/6dd61819859d65af85ef462c7fb694ecbfd2640aa62f41ff0e27e5bb8073f48c.png)\n\nThe function $a(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of ${a}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $a(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $11.$\n\nGiven that $a(5)=-4,$ what is the minimum value ${a}_{\\text{min}}$ of $a(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]?$\n\n${a}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519593 1072 #uuid "e747dd9d-512a-59fa-9944-1137ebe8b2f9" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519593 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $a(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${a}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${a}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $a$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(5) & =-4 \\\\ a(-2) & =a(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-2}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =a(5)-{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4-(-11) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $a$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $a$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{7,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $a$ is ${a}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $a(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519472 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519594 1069 "q-29307" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519594 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519594 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{ab}}.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519594 1072 #uuid "e789ca07-9ab5-56ec-aa69-c4d893a03b51" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519594 1074 "First, we apply the quotient rule and put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{ab}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{ab}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{ab}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{ab},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{ab}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{ab}}{\\sqrt{ab}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2ab}}{(\\sqrt{ab})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2ab}}{ab}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519494 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519595 1069 "q-344100" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519595 1071 "![](images/95/955cda48e8e98f46903a471ceefa657a6032cb2e6feab304569a62750eba9825.png)\n\nThe function $r(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,7].$ The graph of ${r}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $r(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$\n\nGiven that $r(7)=6,$ what is the maximum value ${r}_{\\text{max}}$ of $r(x)$ on the interval $[-1,7]?$\n\n${r}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519595 1072 #uuid "ebf67b7c-064d-5c23-8f05-31612119932c" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519595 1074 "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $r(x)$ on $[-1,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${r}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,7),$ and ${r}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $r$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(7) & =6 \\\\ r(-1) & =r(7)+{\\int }_{7}^{-1}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =r(7)-{\\int }_{-1}^{7}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6-(-10) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $r$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $r$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{6,16\\}=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $r$ is ${r}_{\\text{max}}=16,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $r(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519492 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519596 1069 "q-67317" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519596 1071 "An arithmetic sequence has ${a}_{20}=21.5$ and ${a}_{24}=12.5.$ What is the common difference of the sequence?" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519596 1072 #uuid "ee47bebb-a1a4-5edd-a0dd-08fb92af0f27" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519596 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\n{a}_{20}=21.5,\\,{a}_{24}=12.5.\n$$\n\nTo find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=24$ and $m=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{24}-{a}_{20}}{24-20} \\\\  & =\\frac{12.5-21.5}{24-20} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9}{4} \\\\  & =-2.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-2.25.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519438 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519597 1069 "q-146410" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519597 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<1,y<0.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519597 1072 #uuid "eeeede00-ac86-5992-a122-f787740a8bf0" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519597 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3\\}\\times \\{1,2,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<0,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/8e/8edc6bb45750996ba8d84bf946eaf4de6c68c0f3074ab7af4bee2b68c48c2095.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at least $1$ and $x<1,$ no possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Also, every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $1$ and $y<0,$ so no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$\n\nTherefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ,$ and\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519510 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519598 1069 "q-94003" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519598 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & 0 \\\\ \\,∗\\, & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519598 1072 #uuid "f2f205d5-62a6-56a8-a8b2-ff79782e487c" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519598 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519459 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519599 1069 "q-146847" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519599 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(12,4)=0.6,\\,F(9,4)=0.3,\\,F(3,4)=0.15,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 6,Y\\le 5).$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519599 1072 #uuid "f4402cb5-3b8b-556e-9fb0-20470cdb8422" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519599 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/a7/a782578ef55726c98ed23d89cf3de531ede60fe5db3fa9062c05507c5c2f60af.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(12,4)$ and top-left corner at $(3,4),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/7b/7ba99957a743b5cf283c2668e81b6c1aeb06730d47fc157aec90fe8bc880ced4.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,4)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/3b/3bd652b8d918e26cd330ad43555f039845d0b5405b70c96828bb2d652b7f872c.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6,Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 4)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(12,4)-F(3,4) \\\\  & =0.6-0.15 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519465 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519600 1069 "q-76514" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519600 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519600 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $4$ and the second term is $-2.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519600 1072 #uuid "f4a1a68f-984f-511a-88be-72b335a1e5eb" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519600 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe are given the first term $f(1)=4.$ Also, the common difference of the sequence can be found as\n\n$$\nd=-2-4=-6.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)(-6) \\\\  & =4-6n+6 \\\\  & =10-6n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is $f(n)=10-6n.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519482 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519601 1069 "q-122357" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519601 1071 "![](images/a9/a9c863e0cdb9788e06c527e17207e87ba2281ab21c9bd0836009ca518a15631a.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ rounded to one decimal place." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519601 1072 #uuid "f70a7592-ca8c-59ec-84a8-2fae0d3e16cd" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519601 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{BC}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠C$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ opposite $∠C.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({56}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{9} \\\\ AB & =9\\operatorname{tan}⁡({56}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =9\\operatorname{tan}⁡({56}^{∘}) \\\\  & =9(1.482\\,56…) \\\\  & =13.343\\,04… \\\\  & ≈13.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519433 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519602 1069 "q-95650" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519602 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519602 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519602 1072 #uuid "f78eb475-8e0d-5dc7-9e33-9b189c20d32b" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519602 1074 "The derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519477 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519603 1069 "q-95511" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519603 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y={e}^{4x}-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{e}^{2x},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519603 1072 #uuid "f7c92bc5-8061-56c3-9e0d-1e01c4282d73" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519603 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{4x}-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{4x})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{4x}-2(-\\frac{1}{|{e}^{2x}|\\sqrt{1+({e}^{2x})^{2}}})\\cdot ({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4x}+\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4x}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}} \\\\  & =4({e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519457 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519604 1069 "q-72360" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519604 1071 "![](images/ad/adcf36236abe7d2e691f8341857c9ef19b12821d7e952de0521d1877f510f326.png)\n\nThe figure above shows part of the graph of $y=f(x)$ which is defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,2].$ Given that $f(x)$ is odd, which of the following gives the complete graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519604 1072 #uuid "f7f794d6-627b-530b-9219-5208601d87a7" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519604 1074 "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\nSo, we can obtain the missing part of the graph by rotating the given part ${180}^{∘}$ over the origin. \n\nTherefore, the complete graph of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f096c9cb18025c79f62fc1a8d700fc920e8cfb2c71040a0c03cc2da8b8346c20.png)" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519451 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519605 1069 "q-143633" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519605 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{14}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519605 1072 #uuid "f873ff79-08e6-53a2-8403-85dc4a081919" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519605 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{14}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{14}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{14}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{14}\\cdot [27-(-1)] \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{14})}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{28}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519425 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519606 1069 "q-5274" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519606 1071 "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if its third and sixth terms are $2$ and $-22$, respectively." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519606 1072 #uuid "f9d7919f-152a-59f4-84d8-010f7c33087a" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519606 1074 "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=2,\\,{a}_{6}=-22.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=6$ and $m=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{6}-{a}_{3}}{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-22)-2}{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24}{3} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-8.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519460 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519607 1069 "q-144943" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519607 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3y}{8}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y+1}{8}, & 0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519607 1072 #uuid "fde66fe1-1f42-5587-ae07-4dc1d6de7d02" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519607 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3y}{8})}{(\\frac{3y+1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3y}{3y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3y}{3y+1}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519608 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519626 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519608 1062 #uuid "003b1825-afe9-583d-9089-86405c5e6a74" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519608 1063 #edb/ref 17592186348376 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519609 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519636 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519609 1062 #uuid "01fa0c05-b1ae-5422-bc1f-94b53ddae4b0" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519609 1063 #edb/ref 17592186519526 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519610 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519667 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519610 1062 #uuid "02b0f97c-a1c4-5de3-9e6c-ca0b67ea4390" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519610 1063 #edb/ref 17592186369469 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519611 1062 #uuid "0f8f73fe-f9ae-57fa-a170-226128cdae07" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519611 1063 #edb/ref 17592186404371 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519612 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519658 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519612 1062 #uuid "178605e4-baed-5eae-a9e6-a3614ae150cd" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519612 1063 #edb/ref 17592186311602 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519613 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519611 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519613 1062 #uuid "17b3d78a-4c11-56d5-8c1a-c9703c363db5" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519613 1063 #edb/ref 17592186404364 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519614 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519633 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519614 1062 #uuid "18bfeff9-da8e-5adf-b0b0-f3f134960c7b" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519614 1063 #edb/ref 17592186308467 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519615 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519670 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519615 1062 #uuid "1b44f557-01ab-5482-9558-b2e2cf4a9a80" 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519615 1063 #edb/ref 17592186348389 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519616 1058 #edb/ref 17592186519642 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186519616 1062 #uuid "1cb45d40-3dff-5b26-9cf1-cf5ae9491